hi there steve kaufman here  um i haven't done a video in a while uh  today i'm going to stray a little bit  from the subject of language learning  and talk about education  and and it's still language learning  it's something that caught my attention  it was an article that i saw on the web  i think in linkedin where there's a  language learning  group or community within linkedin and  the subject was that stanford university  is going to  get involved in establishing something  called common core standards for english  and that this is going to help the  you know the increasing number of kids  in north america that's the us and in  canada who are in the school system and  who are not native speakers of english  and who very often struggle  with their uh school work  because of language difficulties and  they're often in esl classes and and so  forth and sometimes they fall behind in  their schoolwork  um and i've mentioned i've given uh some  excerpts and a link to this report at my  blog the linguist on language  but i thought i would add to my  blog report blog post as some comments  the gist of it is that they're going to  have meetings amongst educators and  commissioned papers on  topics about the need to leverage common  core standards for the for english  language proficiency proficiency  development  blah blah blah  and the feeling is that this is going to  be a tremendous benefit  to  these english language learners  i must say i am  totally i cannot understand what it is  they're trying to do i've seen so many  examples here in canada of these  programs over three or four years where  people get together educators teachers  administrators and they're trying to  develop new standards and so forth  we have language benchmarks in canada 12  levels they have the common european  framework in europe which is six levels  everybody is coming up with new  standards new frameworks new tests  um we're investing more and more in  education as i pointed out before in the  united states and it's similar elsewhere  since the late 60s the  cost of educating someone in the school  system has gone up  350 percent  and the results are  the same or possibly worse  when it comes to measurements of  literacy  of knowledge of history and so forth so  that the additional investment and this  is it  after inflation  three and a half times the cost has gone  up and the results are the same or worse  and it's because of this kind of  bureaucratic administrative  jumping through hoops  um  you know to me the issue is how much do  the kids read  every  study that i've seen shows  that the literacy level in other words  how well someone reads is the best  indicator of how well they'll do in  school  and  uh you know rather than having tests and  and of course we've moved away from  uh the kinds of tests where people  actually have to write anything it's a  lot of multiple choice and this kind of  thing the big issue is how do you get  kids to read  uh coming up with new standards for  english even the existing standards a  lot of the kids that are non-native  speakers of english can't even  whatever our standards are now they  can't seem to achieve those standards so  getting higher and more demanding  standards isn't going to do much unless  you get them to read i don't know of  anyone who reads a lot who doesn't also  read well  reading is one of these things the more  you do it the better you get at it the  more words you're exposed to  and so the issue is how do you get  people to read  are there poor people who don't have  access to books maybe  maybe  can we use you know the kindle and such  devices to allow kids to have more  access to books  uh should we allow them  to perhaps have more freedom in choosing  what they're going to read  you know this relates back to language  learning but i spoke to a teenager the  other day here in vancouver who said he  hated french at school because they  never had a chance to read all they did  was study grammar and do tests  that's what he told me and and he was  there with a friend of his who said yeah  that's what we do  i don't think that's the case in all  school systems  but  you know very often i mean i remember  when i was at school  uh we had uh one of many very  uninspiring teachers was droning on  about something  and one of our uh classmates stood up  and  took a put his hand up and said sir  uh can i go to the library and read  well  of course everyone in the class laughed  the teacher turned red in the face and  shouted at him and threw out of the  classroom  but  you know maybe he'd be far better off in  the library reading  things that are of interest to him  and so to me all of this new standards  new tests  what are they going to do here they're  going to meet and and webinars and  sponsor meetings and of course they'll  have donuts and coffee and stuff  it's much simpler  how do you get people to read  and i think that  that reading is very much related to  listening and we have these tremendous  devices now called mp3 players  which can enable you know beginning  readers to hear stories before they  start reading them which makes it easier  for them because they're not familiar  with it  uh  i think we have to say how do we  encourage  people to discover the joys of reading  because once you have that skill it's a  tremendously powerful skill it's like  being able to type quickly  it's one of these skills you have to  invest in in acquiring that skill and  then all kinds of things become possible  you then if you're a good reader  you can actually become an independent  learner at least in an academic sense uh  you know people can learn different  things people can learn you know nature  study and how to make uh machinery and  and and there's many things that you  learn through experience and not  necessarily through reading but reading  is pretty key nowadays  i saw a presentation which indicated  that the  english  level required to read manuals even for  basic  jobs was very very high uh you know math  books even science books are written in  very difficult english if you don't read  well you won't do well in math and  science the core competence is  reading  and so why don't we figure out how do  you get people to read in the classroom  and rather than allowing the teacher to  monopolize so much the time in the  classroom  or  uh trying to engage the students in all  kinds of uh you know make work type uh  you know team learning and uh  you know whatever they do in the  classroom group think type of stuff let  them read let them read in the classroom  if they don't have the habit of reading  at home at least try to cultivate that  habit in the classroom and in that  regard i fully support the ideas of  stephen krashen uh with his extensive  reading let people read and i think that  would simplify uh things and therefore  we wouldn't have to spend millions of  dollars on uh reestablishing common core  state standards for english and all  these other  efforts at creating yet more reports yet  more standards yet more standardized  tests yet more  you know publications reports proposals  that really  so far at least haven't produced any  results because as i said  at the beginning  with an increase of 350 percent in the  cost of k-12 education in the united  states uh the results are actually worse  than they were  uh  30 or so 30 40 years ago so there's my  take on that i i could be very wrong on  this somebody will explain to me what  exactly they are going to do but when i  see this thing about common core  standards for english i just see a lot  more bureaucratic uh  you know  ceremony  which will again produce very little in  the way of improvement in the english  standards of these non-native speakers  who are in the english language school  systems in north america thank you for  listening
 

